                        <a href="/A185150">A185150</a>
                    
                </div>
                <div class=seqname>
                    Number of odd primes p between n^2 and (n+1)^2 with (n/p) = 1, where (-) is the Legendre symbol.
                    
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class=scorerefs>
                        
                        4
            </div>
        </div>
        
            <div>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%I #30 Nov 05 2025 15:22:19
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%S 1,1,2,3,2,2,2,3,3,1,4,2,4,3,5,7,2,3,4,6,5,3,3,4,8,5,4,5,4,4,6,6,6,4,
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%T 9,9,7,7,5,6,7,5,9,5,7,3,9,6,10,6,10,6,8,8,7,7,10,3,12,8,7,10,8,14,11,
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%U 7,10,10,5,9,11,8,7,9,9,18,11,11,12,9,20,6,13,6,10,9,13,9,8,10,10,12,12,6,13,9,12,12,8,23
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%N Number of odd primes p between n^2 and (n&#43;1)^2 with (n/p) = 1, where (-) is the Legendre symbol.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C Conjecture: a(n)&gt;0 for all n&gt;0.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C This is a refinement of Legendre&#39;s conjecture that for each n=1,2,3,... the interval (n^2,(n&#43;1)^2) contains a prime.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C We have verified the conjecture for n up to 10^9.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C Zhi-Wei Sun also made some similar conjectures involving primes and Legendre symbols, below are few examples:
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C (1) If n&gt;10 then there is a prime p between n^2 and (n&#43;1)^2 with (n/p) = ((1-n)/p) = 1. If n&gt;2 is different from 7 and 17, then there is a prime p between n^2 and (n&#43;1)^2 with (n/p) = ((n&#43;1)/p) = 1. If n&gt;1 is not equal to 27, then there is a prime p between n^2 and (n&#43;1)^2 with (n/p) = ((n&#43;2)/p) = 1.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C (2) If n&gt;2 is different from 6, 12, 58, then there is a prime p between n^2 and n^2&#43;n such that (n/p) = 1. If n&gt;20 is not a square, and different from 37 and 77, then there is a prime p between n^2 and n^2&#43;n such that (n/p) = -1.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C (3) For each n=15,16,... there is a prime p between n and 2n such that (n/p) = 1. If n&gt;0 is not a square, then
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%C there is a prime p between n and 2n such that (n/p) = -1.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%H Zhi-Wei Sun, &lt;a href=&#34;/A185150/b185150.txt&#34;&gt;Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000&lt;/a&gt;
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%H Zhi-Wei Sun, &lt;a href=&#34;https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1588&#34;&gt;Conjectures involving primes and quadratic forms&lt;/a&gt;, arXiv:1211.1588.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%e a(10)=1 since 107 is the only prime p between 10^2 and 11^2 with (10/p) = 1.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%t a[n_]:=a[n]=Sum[If[n^2&#43;k&gt;2&amp;&amp;PrimeQ[n^2&#43;k]==True&amp;&amp;JacobiSymbol[n,n^2&#43;k]==1,1,0],{k,1,2n}]
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%t Do[Print[n,&#34; &#34;,a[n]],{n,1,100}]
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%Y Cf. A014085, A185636.
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%K nonn
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%O 1,3
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt>%A _Zhi-Wei Sun_, Dec 29 2012
</tt></p>
            
            <p style="text-indent: -2em; margin-left: 2em; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;"><tt></tt></p>
            
